package cajeta.xpu;

import cajeta.xpu.KernelBuffer;

/**
 * CooperativeMatrix — a tile of a matrix multiply held *cooperatively* by a
 * subgroup (wavefront), mapping to the hardware matrix/tensor cores via
 * `SPV_KHR_cooperative_matrix` (the `OpTypeCooperativeMatrixKHR` family). It is
 * the single biggest ML lever: one `mma` is a full tile-sized fused multiply-add
 * on the matrix-core register file.
 *
 * Parameterized by element type `T` and three compile-time integer constants —
 * `Rows`, `Cols`, and `Use` (the SPIR-V cooperative-matrix "use": 0 = MatrixA,
 * 1 = MatrixB, 2 = Accumulator). A `CooperativeMatrix<float32,16,16,0>` is a
 * 16x16 f32 A-operand tile. Distinct `(T,Rows,Cols,Use)` are distinct device
 * types, exactly as `Vector<T,N>` is per `(T,N)`.
 *
 * Like {@link RayQuery}, a CooperativeMatrix is NOT a host value: it is a
 * device-only, kernel-local object. Its storage is not addressable memory — at
 * Subgroup scope the tile lives *distributed across the wavefront's
 * per-invocation registers* (the matrix-core register file), owned cooperatively
 * by all invocations. You never take its address. Use it inside an `@Kernel`:
 *
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float16,16,16,0> a;
 *     a.load(bufA, 0u, 0u, 16u);             // offset 0, layout 0 = row-major, stride 16
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float16,16,16,1> b;
 *     b.load(bufB, 0u, 0u, 16u);
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float32,16,16,2> c;
 *     c.splat(0.0f);                         // zero accumulator
 *     c.mma(a, b);                           // c = a * b + c  (one tile FMA)
 *     c.store(bufC, 0u, 0u, 16u);
 *
 * That is one tile. A **real GEMM** tiles a larger product: the `offset` and
 * `stride` arguments select a `Rows`x`Cols` sub-tile of a wider matrix, and a
 * K-loop accumulates the partial products into a single accumulator in place.
 * `C[16x16] = A[16x32] · B[32x16]` (two K-tiles) is just a loop — zero the
 * accumulator once, then walk K:
 *
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float32,16,16,2> acc;
 *     acc.splat(0.0f);
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float16,16,16,0> ta;
 *     CooperativeMatrix<float16,16,16,1> tb;
 *     for (int32 kt = 0; kt < 2; kt = kt + 1) {
 *         ta.load(a, kt * 16, 0, 32);     // A sub-tile: offset kt*16, stride 32
 *         tb.load(b, kt * 256, 0, 16);    // B sub-tile: offset kt*16 rows, stride 16
 *         acc.mma(ta, tb);                // acc += ta · tb  (K-accumulation)
 *     }
 *     acc.store(c, 0, 0, 16);
 *
 * Note the A stride (32) is the full row width of the wider matrix, NOT the tile
 * width — that is what makes `load` gather a 16x16 window out of a larger
 * row-major matrix. The M/N *output* dimensions tile the same way: launch one
 * workgroup (one wave) per 16x16 output tile, read `Workgroup.x()` to decode the
 * tile this wave owns (`ti = block / nTilesN`, `tj = block % nTilesN` — every
 * lane of the wave agrees), and offset that wave's A row-band / B col-band / C
 * tile accordingly. A full `C[32x32] = A[32x32] · B[32x32]` is then a 2x2 grid of
 * waves, each running the K-loop above. The complete M/N/K-tiled GEMM is
 * device-verified on the native matrix cores (gfx1151 WMMA).
 *
 * `load`/`store` also have a **`Shared<T>` (LDS) overload** — the classic
 * LDS-staged GEMM optimization: the workgroup stages a panel of A/B into
 * workgroup-shared memory ONCE per K-step (see `CoopStage.panel`), barriers, and
 * then every wave loads its operand tiles out of the shared panel — turning N
 * redundant global reads into one global read plus N cheap LDS reads (the reuse
 * that keeps the matrix cores fed). `load(Shared<T>, ...)` addresses a sub-tile
 * inside the LDS tile exactly as the `KernelBuffer<T>` overload does for global; the
 * matrix VALUE still never leaves the register file. The full staged GEMM
 * (`CoopStage.panel` → `Barrier.workgroup` → `load(Shared)` → `mma`, multiple
 * waves sharing the panels) is device-verified on **all three GPU-capable
 * backends** — AMD (gfx1151 WMMA), the CPU (software tile-matmul over a per-block
 * buffer; the barrier lowers via CPU loop fission), and **Vulkan** (RADV
 * cooperative-matrix loading from Workgroup storage). The Vulkan path required two
 * SPIR-V backend fixes in the cajeta-llvm fork — keeping undef non-constant array
 * globals correctly typed, and access-chaining an aggregate cooperative-matrix
 * pointer to its first element (see cajeta-llvm/UPSTREAM-PRS.md, PR 4 + PR 5).
 * Double-
 * buffering (a second LDS panel staged while the current one computes) is a
 * kernel-authoring pattern on top of these same verbs; a fully-managed tiled-GEMM
 * block is a possible later convenience. For correctness
 * the A and B `Use`/shape must
 * form a valid product into this accumulator; v1 targets the square
 * Rows == Cols == K tile (so A is `<...,0>` and B is `<...,1>` of the same
 * `<T,Rows,Cols>`).
 *
 * ## Runs on every backend — native cores or a portable fallback (CM6)
 *
 * A CooperativeMatrix is **not** Vulkan-only. The same `@Kernel` takes the
 * fastest path each backend offers, chosen statically per `(backend, dtype)`:
 *
 *   - **Native** — the hardware matrix cores. On Vulkan that is
 *     `SPV_KHR_cooperative_matrix` (`OpCooperativeMatrix*KHR`) for the dtype
 *     configs the driver advertises: **float16** and **8-bit integer**
 *     multiplicands (with f32 / i32 accumulators). On AMD it maps to RDNA3
 *     **WMMA** — `v_wmma_f32_16x16x16_{f16,bf16}` (f32 accumulator) and
 *     `v_wmma_i32_16x16x16_iu8` (**int8** multiplicands, i32 accumulator — the
 *     quantized-inference config) for a 16x16x16 tile, device-verified on
 *     gfx1151/RDNA3.5 (the lowerer marshals the per-lane fragment by hand —
 *     int8 packed 4-per-i32 — and compiles the kernel wave32). So **bf16
 *     matmul is native on AMD** even though it is the software tier on Vulkan.
 *   - **Software** — a portable flat tile-matmul: a `[Rows*Cols x T]` tile, a
 *     strided row/col-major gather, and an honest triple-loop `C = A·B`
 *     accumulated in f32 (the bf16/f16 storage-plus-f32-compute model). Used on
 *     the CPU, and on any backend that exposes no matrix config for the dtype —
 *     most notably **bfloat16 on Vulkan** (no driver advertises a bf16
 *     cooperative-matrix config; it would need Intel-only bf16 arithmetic). The
 *     result is bit-identical; it just isn't matrix-core accelerated.
 *
 * The two tiers are interchangeable through the same `load`/`splat`/`mma`/`store`
 * verbs, so a kernel is written once. **All three tiles of one `mma` must share a
 * tier** (give a bf16 GEMM a bf16 accumulator, an f16 GEMM an f32 accumulator) —
 * mixing native and software operands is a compile error.
 *
 * ### How you learn which tier you got — a sticky, non-dissuading `note`
 *
 * When a tile takes the **software** path the compiler emits, once per GEMM,
 * a `note: [mma-tiering]` — a diagnostic severity **below** `warning` (a warning
 * would wrongly read as "avoid this"). It is a capability statement, not a
 * defect: the path is correct, and it auto-promotes to the matrix cores on
 * hardware that exposes the config. Example (compiling a bf16 GEMM):
 * ```
 * note: [mma-tiering] CooperativeMatrix<bfloat16,16,16> runs on the portable
 *   software tile-matmul on the vulkan backend (it exposes no native
 *   cooperative-matrix config for bfloat16). The result is identical; it
 *   automatically uses the hardware matrix cores on backends that do expose the
 *   config (e.g. bf16 WMMA on AMD).
 * ```
 * See docs/LintRules.md § Notes for the `note:` severity.
 *
 * The method bodies are resolution placeholders — never emitted as real calls.
 * Each is lowered at its `@Kernel` call site to the chosen tier (the native
 * backend cooperative-matrix op, or the software tile-matmul). Using a
 * CooperativeMatrix on the host is unsupported.
 */
public final class CooperativeMatrix<T, uint32 Rows, uint32 Cols, uint32 Use> {
    // Placeholder field. CooperativeMatrix is a device-only opaque (the device
    // lowerer ignores this host class and allocates an OpTypeCooperativeMatrixKHR
    // slot), but the host front-end still lays the class out — a zero-field class
    // has no LLVM type. One reserved word gives it a valid (never-used) layout.
    //
    // EVERY VERB BELOW IS `@Intrinsic` AND HAS NO BODY — a declaration with a
    // semicolon. There is nothing to write in one: the kernel lowering
    // intercepts each call by receiver type and method name and emits the
    // instruction at the call site. The empty `{ }` bodies that used to sit
    // here were the hole — from host code they compiled, ran, and did nothing.
    // Now a host call is refused by name (MethodCallExpression), and a kernel's
    // `CooperativeMatrix<…> acc;` is a bare declaration the lowering handles,
    // untouched. Julian, 2026-09-21: "I'd rather have a form where we're not
    // using an empty method body. Just a semicolon terminator."
    //
    // `final` on each verb as well as on the class — belt and braces against
    // anyone supplying a body by overriding, which would silently shadow the
    // lowering's instruction with host code. Not `abstract`: an intrinsic is
    // not an unfilled slot waiting for a subclass, so the class must not read
    // as abstract; CajetaClass::hasAbstractMethod skips intrinsics. Julian,
    // 2026-09-21: "keep it as an annotation and use final to keep the
    // developer from trying to extend it".
    int32 reserved;

    public CooperativeMatrix() {
        this.reserved = 0;
    }

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: load this tile from `src` (a global KernelBuffer<T>),
     * starting at element `offset` and reading a `Rows`x`Cols` tile with the
     * given `layout` (0 = row-major, 1 = column-major) and row/column `stride`
     * (in elements). `offset` selects a sub-tile of a larger matrix: for a
     * row-major M×K matrix, tile (ti,tj) starts at `(ti*Rows)*K + tj*Cols` with
     * stride `K` — the addressing a tiled GEMM walks over.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void load(KernelBuffer<T> src, uint32 offset, uint32 layout, uint32 stride);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: load this tile from a workgroup-shared (`Shared<T>`,
     * i.e. LDS) tile instead of a global `KernelBuffer<T>` — the consume side of the
     * LDS-staged GEMM. The workgroup stages a panel global→LDS once (see
     * `CoopStage.panel`), barriers, and then every wave loads its operand tile out
     * of that shared panel; `offset`/`layout`/`stride` address the sub-tile inside
     * the LDS tile exactly as the `KernelBuffer<T>` overload does for global memory.
     * Same fragment layout and tier rules — only the source storage class differs.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void load(Shared<T> src, uint32 offset, uint32 layout, uint32 stride);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: broadcast `value` across every element of this tile
     * — the zero/initial accumulator (`OpCompositeConstruct` of a single scalar).
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void splat(T value);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: fused multiply-add this accumulator tile in place —
     * `this = a * b + this` — one matrix-core MMA. `a` is the MatrixA operand
     * (Use 0), `b` the MatrixB operand (Use 1); `this` is the accumulator (Use 2).
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void mma(CooperativeMatrix<T, Rows, Cols, 0> a,
                    CooperativeMatrix<T, Rows, Cols, 1> b);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — the fused GEMM-epilogue verb
     * (xpu-coopmatrix-epilogue, Option B): for every element of this
     * ACCUMULATOR tile (`Use = 2`, int32 or float32),
     *
     *     facc[r][c] += (rowF[r] * colF[c]) * this[r][c]
     *
     * `facc` is a same-shape `CooperativeMatrix<float32, Rows, Cols, 2>`;
     * `rowF`/`colF` are `Shared<float32>` vectors of `Rows`/`Cols`
     * elements. One fma chain per element with exactly that grouping on
     * EVERY tier — the consumers' acceptance is bit-exact agreement, so
     * the association is part of the contract, not an implementation
     * detail. On amdgpu-native this runs in the accumulator fragment's
     * registers (no LDS round trip, no barrier); the software tile runs
     * the elementwise loop; a native tier without epilogue support
     * demotes the kernel's tiles to the portable tile, loudly.
     *
     * This is the k-quant dequant seam: fold `d * sc` into `rowF` per
     * K-slice, the activation scale into `colF`, and the int32 mma
     * accumulator flows into a register-resident float accumulator
     * without ever being spilled.
     *
     * Every Shared-vector argument of the epilogue verbs also accepts
     * a SLICE, spelled `arr[offset]` (10.12.33): the vector is read
     * starting at that element, so per-wave or per-tile vectors can
     * live in one Shared array selected by offset instead of one
     * array per wave behind a wave-uniform branch.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void scaledAccumInto(CooperativeMatrix<float32, Rows, Cols, 2> facc,
                                Shared<float32> rowF, Shared<float32> colF);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — the rank-1 epilogue accumulate
     * (xpu-coopmatrix-epilogue, Option B): on a `float32` accumulator
     * tile (`Use = 2`),
     *
     *     this[r][c] += rowF[r] * colF[c]
     *
     * The separable correction term of min-based quant formats (Q4_K's
     * `-dmin·m` sub-block term, folded per K-slice the way llama.cpp
     * folds it). Same tier and grouping rules as {@link #scaledAccumInto}.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void rank1Accum(Shared<float32> rowF, Shared<float32> colF);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — {@link #rank1Accum} with the column vector
     * supplied as a {@link WaveVector} (4A.5.4.C): `ofSlice` reads it from a
     * Shared panel, `broadcast` uses one factor for every column, `ofLane`
     * takes a per-lane register value (native-only).
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void rank1Accum(Shared<float32> rowG,
                           WaveVector<float32, Cols> colG);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — the DUAL fused epilogue
     * (xpu-coopmatrix-epilogue follow-on, threaded-forward-path
     * 10.12.21): one per-element pass computing
     *
     *     facc[r][c] += (rowF[r] * colF[c]) * this[r][c]
     *                 +  rowG[r] * colG[c]
     *
     * — the k-quant scale seam AND its separable min correction in a
     * single accumulator visit. Same tier, shape and grouping rules as
     * {@link #scaledAccumInto}.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void scaledAccumInto2(CooperativeMatrix<float32, Rows, Cols, 2> facc,
                                 Shared<float32> rowF, Shared<float32> colF,
                                 Shared<float32> rowG, Shared<float32> colG);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — {@link #scaledAccumInto2} with the two column
     * vectors supplied as {@link WaveVector}s (4A.5.4.C); each column may be built by `ofSlice`,
     * `broadcast`, or `ofLane` independently.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void scaledAccumInto2(CooperativeMatrix<float32, Rows, Cols, 2> facc,
                                 Shared<float32> rowF, WaveVector<float32, Cols> colF,
                                 Shared<float32> rowG, WaveVector<float32, Cols> colG);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — {@link #scaledAccumInto} with the column
     * factors supplied as a {@link WaveVector} rather than a whole Shared
     * vector (4A.5.4.C). Build it with `ofSlice` (reads the factors from a
     * Shared panel, per column, on EVERY tier), `broadcast` (one factor for
     * all columns), or `ofLane` (a per-lane register value — native-only).
     *
     * CONTRACT — read this as tile algebra, not as a fragment layout. The
     * wave collectively supplies the whole column vector: lane L supplies the
     * factor for column `L mod Cols`, and each backend redistributes as its
     * fragment requires (on AMD the lane owns its column; on NVIDIA a lane's
     * eight accumulator elements span four columns, so the lowering recovers
     * column `c`'s factor for each). `rowF` stays a Shared vector because rows
     * are genuinely cross-lane. Say which SLICE OF THE TILE a lane supplies,
     * never which registers it owns — the old `lane & 15` wording was AMD's
     * fragment layout leaking into a portable API, and it shipped eleven
     * kernels that were right in column 0 and wrong everywhere else.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void scaledAccumInto(CooperativeMatrix<float32, Rows, Cols, 2> facc,
                                Shared<float32> rowF,
                                WaveVector<float32, Cols> colF);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic — the INTEGER accumulate: on an `int32`
     * accumulator tile (`Use = 2`), for every element of the tile,
     *
     *     iacc[r][c] += colS[c] * this[r][c]
     *
     * `iacc` is a same-shape `CooperativeMatrix<int32, Rows, Cols, 2>` and
     * `colS` a {@link WaveVector}: lane L supplies the factor for column
     * `L mod Cols`. Build it with `ofSlice` — sourcing the per-column int
     * scales from a Shared panel lets the PORTABLE software tile run this too
     * (the replicated tile reads column c's factor directly) — or `broadcast`;
     * `ofLane` is native-only. This folds a small integer per-K-slice scale
     * into an int32 accumulator so the float drain runs ONCE per outer block
     * instead of once per sub-block: no convert, no fma, no rank-1 term per
     * slice.
     *
     * CONTRACT — 24 BITS: `colS` and every element of the receiver must lie
     * in [-2^23, 2^23). The lowering is the full-rate 24-bit multiply (a
     * 32-bit multiply is quarter-rate and gives the epilogue back), so a wider
     * operand is silently truncated. Q4_K is the motivating case and fits by
     * construction: a 32-K int8 dot is at most 32*127*127 in magnitude and the
     * 6-bit sub-block scale is at most 63, so the product stays well inside
     * 24 bits.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void scaledAccumI32(CooperativeMatrix<int32, Rows, Cols, 2> iacc,
                               WaveVector<int32, Cols> colS);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: feed an int8 A or B operand tile straight
     * from packed registers (xpu-coopmatrix-fromwords).
     *
     * CONTRACT — tile algebra, like {@link #scaledAccumInto}, and for
     * the same reason. The wave collectively supplies the whole tile:
     *
     *     lane L supplies row (`Use` 0) or column (`Use` 1)
     *     `L mod 16`, as its sixteen K-values k = 0..15 in
     *     little-endian bytes — `w0` carries k 0..3, `w1` k 4..7,
     *     `w2` k 8..11, `w3` k 12..15.
     *
     * Lanes sharing an `L mod 16` present the same words, which they do
     * by construction when the words derive from `L mod 16`. Nothing
     * here says which registers the lane's fragment occupies: on AMD
     * the iu8 operand IS four i32s per lane and the words go straight
     * in, while on NVIDIA the fragment is an opaque `{i32 x 2}` and the
     * lowering stages the words through one shared slice per wave and
     * re-loads the tile.
     *
     * This is what lets a k-quant GEMM widen nibbles straight into the
     * matrix-core operand: a `load` can only read a fragment from
     * memory, so an in-kernel widen had to round-trip LDS with a
     * barrier on each side — measured at ~3x the tile rate of a feed
     * that reads pre-widened bytes. With `fromWords` the lane reads its
     * own 16 packed bytes, extracts, and presents the fragment.
     *
     * NATIVE-ONLY. A tier with no wave to distribute across — the
     * replicated software tile, whose every work-item holds the whole
     * matrix — REJECTS the kernel with a named diagnostic rather than
     * quietly taking one lane's words for the whole tile. Only the
     * 16x16 int8 operand shapes (`Use` 0 or 1) are accepted.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void fromWords(int32 w0, int32 w1, int32 w2, int32 w3);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: store this tile to `dst` (a global KernelBuffer<T>),
     * starting at element `offset`, with the given `layout` and `stride` — the
     * write-side counterpart of `load`'s sub-tile addressing.
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void store(KernelBuffer<T> dst, uint32 offset, uint32 layout, uint32 stride);

    /**
     * Device-only intrinsic: store this tile to a workgroup-shared (`Shared<T>`,
     * i.e. LDS) tile — the write-side counterpart of the `Shared<T>` load. Useful
     * for a coalesced epilogue (stage the accumulator tile to LDS, then have the
     * workgroup flush it to global in a coalesced pattern).
     */
    @Intrinsic
    public final void store(Shared<T> dst, uint32 offset, uint32 layout, uint32 stride);
}
